WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.480
Welcome to unintended consequences, Episode 5, for May 11, 2026. Starting the week with a case

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:14.280
study you'll be thinking about for days. In January 1920, the day after the 18th amendment

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:20.900
banned the manufacture and sale of intoxicating liquor, Chicago police reported that saloons had

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:27.360
simply shuttered their front doors. New suppliers began delivering whiskey by the case from hidden

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:34.540
stills. Reformers had passed the amendment to shrink crime, cut family violence, and empty jails.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.080
Within three years, the same city saw weekly shootouts between rival liquor gangs

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.440
and an estimated 30,000 speakeasies operating openly. The policy that aimed to dry up the nation

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:54.240
instead built the largest criminal enterprises the United States had ever known. The 18th amendment

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.720
grew out of a century-long temperance movement that viewed alcohol as the root cause of poverty,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:08.120
domestic abuse, and industrial accidents. Organizations such as the Anti-Saloon League and the Women's

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.900
Christian Temperance Union collected petitions and election data showing that saloon districts

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:22.920
produced the highest rates of assault and child neglect. By 1917, more than half the states had

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.220
already adopted some form of prohibition. This gave national leaders the impression that a

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:36.580
constitutional ban was simply extend a proven local success. Leaders like Wayne Wheeler of the

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:43.920
Anti-Saloon League argued that removing legal alcohol would free working families from weekly wage losses.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.840
It would also reduce the number of men showing up drunk at factory gates. At the time,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:58.060
the United States was entering World War I. Green conservation plus anti-German sentiment against

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:05.700
brewers made the ban appear both patriotic and practical. Supporters therefore saw the amendment

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:13.420
not as an experiment in social engineering but as the logical next step after decades of state-level

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:21.960
evidence. Congress passed the Volsted Act in October 1919 to define intoxicating beverages as anything

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:31.620
over 0.5% alcohol. It also created federal enforcement machinery. The 18th amendment itself was

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:40.240
ratified on January 16, 1919, and took effect one year later. Early reports from dry counties showed

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.420
sharp drops in public intoxication arrests. In the first six months of national prohibition,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.580
some cities recorded fewer than half the previous years drunk and disorderly cases.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:59.120
Prohibition advocates pointed to these numbers as proof the law was working. Skeptics, including

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:05.220
several big city police chiefs, warned that enforcement would require far more agents than Congress

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:12.160
had funded. They also warned that the Canadian and Caribbean borders would become pipelines for smuggled

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:20.320
liquor. The federal government initially assigned only 1,500 agents to patrol the entire country.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.580
That number remained roughly constant through the decade because legal production and sale ended

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:33.980
while demand stayed high. A new market opened for anyone willing to supply alcohol outside the law.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:42.200
In Chicago, Johnny Torio and his lieutenant Al Capone organized breweries and distribution routes that

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:51.660
generated roughly $100 million a year by 1927. Similar syndicates formed in New York under Lucky

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:59.300
Luciano. Others formed in Detroit along the river from Windsor, Ontario. The sudden concentration

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:07.820
of enormous cash flows rewarded violence. The St Valentine's Day massacre of 1929, in which seven

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:14.660
men were machine gunned in a garage, was only the most publicized episode. It fit a pattern of

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:21.640
territorial killings that claimed hundreds of lives. At the same time, the disappearance of regulated

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:27.660
distilleries meant much of the available liquor came from industrial alcohol diverted and then

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:34.040
cleaned with toxic additives. The federal government continued to require methanol and other

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:41.100
poisons in industrial stocks. Estimates from the Treasury Department later placed the number of deaths

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:48.160
from poison liquor in the thousands each year. Ordinary citizens who had never bought illegal alcohol

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:55.720
before now entered speakeasies. This eroded respect for the law itself. Police departments found

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:02.060
themselves outgunned and underpaid relative to the bribes available. That produced widespread

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:09.300
corruption that reached city halls and federal offices. Rural still operators in the Appalachian

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:15.520
counties expanded production. The resulting moonshine sometimes reached 150 proof.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:22.640
This increased the rate of alcohol poisoning even among those who avoided industrial contaminants.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:30.000
The policy therefore did not merely fail to reduce crime. It shifted criminal activity from

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.440
scattered saloon brawls to disciplined armed organizations that controlled entire cities.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:44.800
By 1930, both major parties recognized that enforcement costs and public defiance had become unsustainable.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:53.400
Franklin Roosevelt campaigned in 1932 on a repeal platform. The 21st amendment was ratified on

00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:02.220
December 5, 1933. This ended national prohibition after 13 years. States regained the power to

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:09.840
regulate alcohol. Most quickly adopted systems of licensed sales and taxation that generated revenue

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:16.800
during the depression. The criminal empires did not disappear overnight. Many simply moved into

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:23.200
gambling and labor racketeering. But the specific incentive to control liquor distribution

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:29.300
vanished. Public health data collected after repeal showed a rapid decline in deaths from

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.560
denatured alcohol. Prohibition illustrates how an intervention that removes a legal market without

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:44.040
shrinking underlying demand reliably creates a black market. Participants in that market face strong

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:51.500
incentives toward violence and product adulteration. Complex social behaviors such as drinking are

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:58.660
embedded in networks of supply, custom, and enforcement capacity. Altering one node without

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:05.240
addressing the others tends to shift harms rather than eliminate them. Modern efforts to restrict

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:11.880
other substances or behaviors therefore benefit from asking whether legal channels can be shaped

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:19.220
rather than simply closed. When policymakers consider new bans today, the question worth testing

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:26.200
is whether the expected reduction in use will exceed the predictable growth of unregulated suppliers

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:33.720
and the enforcement burden those suppliers impose. That's unintended consequences for today.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:39.160
If this episode gave you something to think about, share it with a friend who'd appreciate the story.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.460
Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll be back tomorrow with another case. This episode used

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:51.840
AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
